\chapter{Effects, errors, and optimization contracts}
Optimization correctness includes values, visible effects, and failure behavior.
MuLL numbers are finite binary64 values, collections are immutable values, and
calls may mutate captured cells or invoke arbitrary host services. A rewrite
that looks algebraically reasonable can therefore be observably wrong.

\begin{mullcode}
var calls <- 0;
val next <- fn() { calls <- calls + 1; return calls; };
G:Assert((true and next()) == 1);
G:Assert((false and next()) == false);
G:Assert((nil or next()) == 2);
G:Assert(calls == 2);
G:Assert((1e16 + -1e16) + 1 == 1);
G:Assert(1e16 + (-1e16 + 1) == 0);
\end{mullcode}

\section{What the passes can prove}
Literal short-circuit conditions determine which operand is evaluated without
consulting host state. Unresolved names and calls cannot be assumed pure.
Even \api{x + 0} is not generally an identity: x may be a string, list, nil,
or an invalid numeric result. Numeric literals retain their original spelling
and evaluator validation. No pass moves a callback across another callback.

\section{Diagnostics and state}
Parsing and MuLLIER's static checks precede effects for the entire compilation
unit. Evaluation failures retain earlier effects. Import failures are converted
to nil and recorded in \api{lastError}; failed imports can be retried. A successful
import, including a cache hit, clears that message. String \api{$E?} expansion
is an evaluation-time operation, so string literals are not globally folded.

\section{Resource accounting}
Optimization can reduce interpreter steps. The evaluator's maximum-step setting
is a work bound, not a promise that optimized and unoptimized executions consume
the same count. Differential tests compare results and effects with sufficient
budgets, then independently exercise exhaustion. E-graph iteration, node, and
match bounds constrain saturation; source size and parser nesting bound inputs.
Large native operations remain subject to their own contracts.

\section{Verification strategy}
Toolkit tests cover parsing, matching, congruence, extraction, and serialization.
Host tests cover pass invocation, source sites, rule reload, budget exhaustion,
and pre-effect diagnostics. Shared fixtures exercise language semantics, all
new libraries, and complete \api{mullcode} listings. Running these in both host
languages and both optimizer modes catches differences invisible to a single
implementation's unit tests.
